sources.prompt:
Create the OPPOSITE FACING version of the attached 4×4 furniture atlas, for the other wall of an isometric room.

CRITICAL GEOMETRY CHANGE: Reflect the GEOMETRY OF EACH INDIVIDUAL OBJECT horizontally within its own cell. Keep the 16 objects in the exact same cells and order. The desk, turntable console and record cabinet currently have their long front edges rising towards the RIGHT; in this new drawing those long front edges MUST SLOPE DOWN TOWARDS THE RIGHT by about 25 degrees. The far corner of each tabletop now sits at upper-left, the near corner at lower-right. Show the opposite narrow side panel of the cabinet. This must be visibly the other isometric orientation, suitable for facing a different wall. Do not keep the current camera orientation. Do not rotate flat images by 90 degrees. Do not mirror the entire atlas (that would scramble cell order).

Repaint shading for light from UPPER LEFT in SCREEN SPACE, even though object geometry reverses. Preserve the honey oak, forest-green fabric/enamel and cream/brass palette, beautiful tactile crafted pixel look and all details. Empty clock dial, calendar, weather display, task papers and books remain completely BLANK (no lettering/numbers). The desk drawer cabinet swaps screen side. Turntable tonearm and hinges must be reconstructed correctly for this opposite view.

Maintain 4 rows × 4 columns with roomy transparent margins. EXACT SAME ORDER as reference:
row1 turntable console cabinet, empty writing desk, chair, tall bookshelf;
row2 speaker, vinyl storage cabinet, empty pet bed, potted plant;
row3 blank clock, empty task board, blank calendar, blank weather station;
row4 open blank lyrics book, open blank notebook with pen, wall letter holder, desk lamp.
Everything isolated on TRUE TRANSPARENT RGBA, no background, no ground plane, no pets, no text. Make it clearly a second sprite orientation, never a duplicate of the original perspective.
